Exception Handling

Exception Handling prevents a program from terminating unexpectedly when runtime errors occur.

try and catch
try block contains code that may generate an exception.
catch block handles the exception.
Syntax
try {
    int result = 10 / 0;
}
catch (ArithmeticException e) {
    System.out.println("Cannot divide by zero");
}
throw

The throw keyword is used to explicitly throw an exception.

Example
if(age < 18){
    throw new ArithmeticException("Not Eligible");
}
throws

The throws keyword declares that a method may throw one or more exceptions.

Example
public void readFile() throws IOException{
    // code
}
Java Collections Framework

The Java Collections Framework provides classes and interfaces to store and manipulate groups of objects.

ArrayList

An ArrayList is a dynamic array.

Features
Fast random access/searching
Maintains insertion order
Allows duplicate elements
Example
ArrayList<String> list = new ArrayList<>();
list.add("Java");
list.add("Spring");
LinkedList

A LinkedList stores elements as nodes.

Features
Faster insertion and deletion
Sequential access
Dynamic size
Example
LinkedList<String> list = new LinkedList<>();
HashSet

A HashSet stores only unique elements.

Features
No duplicate values
Unordered collection
Fast lookup
Example
HashSet<Integer> set = new HashSet<>();
set.add(10);
set.add(20);
HashMap

A HashMap stores data as Key–Value pairs.

Features
Keys are unique
Values may be duplicated
Fast retrieval using keys
Example
HashMap<Integer,String> map = new HashMap<>();

map.put(101,"John");
map.put(102,"David");
Queue

A Queue follows the FIFO (First In First Out) principle.

The element inserted first is removed first.

Example
Queue<Integer> queue = new LinkedList<>();

queue.add(10);
queue.add(20);

queue.poll();
Priority Queue

A Priority Queue processes elements according to their priority rather than insertion order.

Higher-priority elements are served first.

Example
PriorityQueue<Integer> pq = new PriorityQueue<>();

pq.add(50);
pq.add(10);
pq.add(30);

Output order:

10
30
50

(Default: smallest element has highest priority.)

File Handling

Java provides classes to read and write files.

Main operations:

Read data from files
Write data to files
Append data
Delete files
Writing into a File
import java.io.FileWriter;

public class Demo {

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {

        FileWriter writer = new FileWriter("sample.txt");

        writer.write("Hello Infosys!");

        writer.close();
    }
}
Reading a File
FileReader reader = new FileReader("sample.txt");
Multithreading

Multithreading allows multiple tasks to execute simultaneously.

Advantages
Faster execution
Better CPU utilization
Improved responsiveness
Example
Thread t = new Thread();

t.start();
JDBC (Java Database Connectivity)

JDBC allows Java applications to communicate with relational databases.

Steps
Load Driver
Establish Connection
Create Statement
Execute Query
Close Connection
JDBC Connection Example
Connection con = DriverManager.getConnection(
    "jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/company",
    "root",
    "password"
);
SQL Database vs NoSQL Database
SQL Database
Structured data
Uses tables
Fixed schema
Example:
MySQL
Oracle
PostgreSQL
NoSQL Database
Unstructured or semi-structured data
Flexible schema
Stores JSON-like documents

Examples:

MongoDB
Cassandra
Firebase
Java Annotations

Annotations provide metadata to the compiler or runtime.

They help reduce configuration and improve code readability.

Common Java Annotations
1. @Override

Indicates that a method overrides a superclass method.

@Override
public void display() {

}
2. @Deprecated

Marks a method or class as obsolete.

@Deprecated
public void oldMethod(){

}
3. @SuppressWarnings

Suppresses compiler warnings.

@SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
4. @FunctionalInterface

Declares that an interface contains exactly one abstract method.

@FunctionalInterface
interface Demo{

    void show();
}
